 "intro": [
  "After the twelve sons of Israel (2:1–2), the Chronicler turns first to Judah, the royal tribe. Through Perez and Hezron the line divides into three clans: Jerahmeel, Ram (the line of David) and Caleb (here Chelubai, 2:9). Many \"sons\" are towns and districts of Judah: \"father of\" often means founder or leader of a place (Mesha \"the father of Ziph,\" Salma \"the father of Bethlehem\").",
  "Dashed lines mark links that the text implies but does not state."
 ],
 "sources": "Hebrew names: Miqra according to the Masorah. Rashi and the Targum on 1 Chronicles 2; Babylonian Talmud, Sotah 11b. Scholarly summary after the standard commentaries (Japhet; Williamson; Knoppers).",
